        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe new `FiscalYear` class is well-placed and idiomatic: it follows the model layer's established \"single source of truth\" derivation pattern (private constructor, static `of(Owner)`, javadoc that cross-links `MembershipNumber`/`MembershipLevel`), and both `MembershipLevel.tenureFiscalYears` and `MembershipNumber.of` correctly route the *year computation* through `FiscalYear.startYearOf`. One structural issue remains.\n\n**Duplicated two-digit fiscal-year rendering (`FiscalYear.java:46` / `MembershipNumber.java:22`)**\n\nThe expression that renders the two-digit fiscal start year \u2014 `String.format(\"%02d\", startYearOf(...) % 100)` \u2014 now lives in two places:\n\n- `FiscalYear.label`: `String.format(\"FY%02d\", startYearOf(date) % 100)`\n- `MembershipNumber.of`: `\"-M\" + String.format(\"%02d\", FiscalYear.startYearOf(...) % 100)`\n\n`MembershipNumber` delegates the *which fiscal year* decision to `FiscalYear` but re-implements the `% 100` + `%02d` two-digit formatting that `FiscalYear.label` also owns. In this codebase specifically this cuts against the grain \u2014 the model layer repeatedly centralizes exactly this kind of formatting so the \"producing and parsing sides cannot drift apart\" (see `CustomerCode.SEPARATOR`, `OwnerIdentity` normalization). The `FiscalYear` javadoc even bills it as *the* single source of truth for fiscal-year derivations, yet the membership number's year segment renders its two digits independently.\n\nSuggested fix: expose the two-digit rendering once on `FiscalYear` and have both callers use it, e.g.\n\n```java\npublic static String twoDigitStartYear(LocalDate date) {\n    return String.format(\"%02d\", startYearOf(date) % 100);\n}\npublic static String label(LocalDate date) {\n    return \"FY\" + twoDigitStartYear(date);\n}\n```\n\nand in `MembershipNumber.of`:\n\n```java\nreturn owner.getCustomerCode() + \"-M\" + FiscalYear.twoDigitStartYear(owner.getRegistrationDate());\n```\n\nMinor, optional: `FiscalYear.label(LocalDate)` is `public` but only called internally by `of(Owner)` (no external callers). If the date-taking overload isn't needed at the edges, consider making it `private` to keep the public surface to the owner-facing `of` + the `startYearOf` primitive the sibling classes consume."
